Microsoft Learn: Configure Virtual Machine Availability

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Virtual Machine Availability
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to design and implement highly available Azure Virtual Machine solutions using Availability Sets, Availability Zones, Virtual Machine Scale Sets (VMSS), and Autoscaling.

This module focused on minimizing downtime, improving resiliency, and automatically scaling Azure Virtual Machines to meet changing workload demands.

Step 1: Understanding High Availability in Azure

The module introduced high availability as the ability to keep applications and services operational during hardware failures, maintenance events, and unexpected outages.

Common causes of downtime:
Hardware failures
Operating system failures
Planned maintenance
Datacenter outages
Increased workload demand
Goals of high availability:
Minimize downtime
Improve reliability
Support business continuity
Increase fault tolerance

The module emphasized that Azure provides multiple technologies to improve VM availability.

Step 2: Planning for Maintenance and Downtime

The training covered how Azure performs platform maintenance.

Microsoft periodically performs:

Hardware updates
Security updates
Infrastructure maintenance

Without planning, maintenance can cause application interruptions.

Key concepts learned:
Infrastructure failures are unavoidable
Applications should be designed for resiliency
Availability solutions reduce maintenance impact

The module highlighted availability planning as a critical administrator responsibility.

Step 3: Understanding Availability Sets

A major focus of the module was Availability Sets.

Availability Sets distribute virtual machines across different physical infrastructure components.

Example:
Availability Set

VM1
VM2
VM3
Benefits:
Protect against hardware failures
Protect against maintenance events
Improve application uptime
Key lessons learned:
Availability Sets work within a single datacenter
Multiple VMs should be deployed inside the same Availability Set
Recommended for applications requiring redundancy
Step 4: Understanding Fault Domains

The training introduced Fault Domains.

A Fault Domain represents a group of hardware that shares a common power source and network switch.

Example:
Fault Domain 1
 └─ VM1

Fault Domain 2
 └─ VM2

Fault Domain 3
 └─ VM3
Benefits:
Hardware failure isolation
Improved resiliency
Reduced risk of application outages
Key concepts learned:
If one Fault Domain fails, other VMs remain available
Availability Sets automatically distribute VMs across Fault Domains

Fault Domains are a frequently tested AZ-104 concept.

Step 5: Understanding Update Domains

The module introduced Update Domains.

Update Domains help minimize downtime during planned Azure maintenance.

Example:
Update Domain 1
 └─ VM1

Update Domain 2
 └─ VM2

Update Domain 3
 └─ VM3

During maintenance:

VM1 Updated
VM2 Remains Online
VM3 Remains Online
Benefits:
Reduced maintenance impact
Improved application availability
Staged infrastructure updates
Key lessons learned:
Azure updates one Update Domain at a time
Not all VMs reboot simultaneously
Step 6: Understanding Availability Zones

A major focus of the module was Availability Zones.

Availability Zones are physically separate datacenters within the same Azure region.

Each zone has:

Independent power
Independent cooling
Independent networking
Example:
East US Region

Zone 1 → VM1
Zone 2 → VM2
Zone 3 → VM3
Benefits:
Datacenter-level fault tolerance
Improved disaster recovery
Higher availability than Availability Sets
Key lessons learned:
Availability Zones protect against entire datacenter failures
Applications remain available if one zone becomes unavailable
Step 7: Availability Sets vs Availability Zones

The training compared the two availability options.

Feature	Availability Set	Availability Zone
Protects Hardware Failures	Yes	Yes
Protects Maintenance Events	Yes	Yes
Protects Datacenter Failure	No	Yes
Scope	Single Datacenter	Multiple Datacenters
Availability Level	High	Highest
Key lesson learned:

Availability Zones provide greater resiliency and are preferred when supported.

This comparison is commonly tested on the AZ-104 exam.

